<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pearl River (Mississippi–Louisiana)</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pearl-river">Pearl River</a> flows about 790 kilometres through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mississippi-river">Mississippi</a> and along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louisiana">Louisiana</a> border into Lake Borgne and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-of-mexico">Gulf of Mexico</a>. Its lower course separated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florida-parishes">Florida Parishes</a>, part of the short-lived Republic of West <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florida">Florida</a> in 1810, from the lands to the east that went to Mississippi and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alabama">Alabama</a>. It still marks part of the boundary between Louisiana and Mississippi.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pearl-river-mississippi-louisiana.jpg" alt="Pearl River (Mississippi–Louisiana)" width="320" height="180" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: J.G.W.Blakeney · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pearl_River_at_Rosemary.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
